### Account unlock — key-material read (uc.account.unlock)
# Runnable acceptance for the authenticated key-material endpoint the client fetches before unlocking the
# Master Key on-device. Run against any running instance (no seed needed: these paths are all pre-auth
# rejections). The server only ever serves ciphertext here, and never to an unauthenticated caller.
#
# The authenticated happy path — a 200 returning the caller's wraps + wrappedIdentityPrivateKey — lives in
# account/authed-flow.http (signed login via ../_lib/ed25519.js; fresh instance), and in the backend slice
# (AccountKeyMaterialSliceTest) + the web unlock test (cr-0004 phase 4). This file covers the no-signature
# boundaries, runnable against ANY instance: the no-anonymous-read and bad-bearer cases, both a generic 401.
#
# Run: ijhttp --env-file http/account/http-client.env.json --env dev http/account/unlock.http

### 1. No bearer token → generic 401 (no anonymous read of key material)
GET {{host}}/api/account/key-material
Accept: application/json

> {%
client.test("anonymous read is a generic 401", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected code authentication_failed");
});
%}

### 2. A bogus bearer token → the SAME generic 401 (unknown token is indistinguishable from no token)
GET {{host}}/api/account/key-material
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer this-token-was-never-issued

> {%
client.test("bogus bearer is a generic 401", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected code authentication_failed");
});
%}

### 3. A malformed Authorization header (no Bearer scheme) → still a generic 401
GET {{host}}/api/account/key-material
Accept: application/json
Authorization: this-is-not-a-bearer-header

> {%
client.test("malformed authorization header is a generic 401", function () {
    client.assert(response.status === 401, "expected 401, got " + response.status);
    client.assert(response.body.code === "authentication_failed", "expected code authentication_failed");
});
%}
